    ];

    agent [label="Agent", fillcolor="#F4A6A6", color="#B23A48"];
    causal [label="Causal Model\nSCM", fillcolor="#A0D6D1", color="#00897B"];
    action [label="Action a", fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B"];
    reward [label="Reward R", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", color="#558B2F"];
    confounder [label="Confounder U", fillcolor="#E8D9F7", color="#9B7DB1"];

    agent -> action [penwidth=1.8];
    causal -> agent [penwidth=1.8];
    action -> reward [penwidth=1.8];
    confounder -> action [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C", penwidth=1.6];
    confounder -> reward [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C", penwidth=1.6];
    action -> causal [label="  learn", penwidth=1.8];
}
```

::::
:::

* From Correlation to Causation in RL

- **Problem**: Standard RL agents optimize over correlations between states,
  actions, and rewards observed during interaction
  - No distinction between _observational_ and _interventional_ distributions
  - Policies may exploit spurious correlations

- **Formalization**: Two distributions with different semantics
  // TODO(gp_ai): Use a 3 aligned
  $$
  \text{Observational}: \quad \EE[R | a] = \sum_r r \cdot \Pr(r | a) \quad \text{(what we see)}
  $$
  $$
  \text{Interventional}: \quad \EE[R | do(a)] = \sum_r r \cdot \Pr(r | \text{forced } a) \quad \text{(what happens if we force } a)
  $$
  - When confounders exist: $\EE[R | a] \neq \EE[R | do(a)]$

- **Solution**: Causal RL represents _why_ an action leads to an outcome
  - Uses the do-operator $do(a)$ to distinguish intervention from observation
  - Builds policies on causal mechanisms, not correlations

- **Key Insight**: Standard MDP formalism assumes no confounding
  - $\Pr(s'|a, s)$ treats all transitions equally, regardless of causality
  - Critical when data comes from sources other than the agent's own exploration

* Confounding in Sequential Decision Making

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- **Definition**: A confounder $U$ is a hidden variable influencing both the
  chosen action and the resulting reward

  - Confounding creates a backdoor path: $U \to A, U \to R$
  - Breaks the inference: $\EE[R | A] \neq \EE[R | do(A)]$

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph Confounding {
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.8;
    ranksep=0.8;
    bgcolor="white";
    compound=true;

    graph [
        fontname="Helvetica",
        pad="0.25"
    ];

    node [
        shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=12,
        penwidth=1.4,
        margin="0.18,0.12"
    ];

    edge [
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=11,
        penwidth=1.4,
        arrowsize=0.8
    ];

    U [label="Hidden Confounder U", fillcolor="#E8D9F7", color="#9B7DB1"];
    A [label="Action a", fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B"];
    R [label="Reward R", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", color="#558B2F"];

    U -> A [label="  influences", color="#D64545", penwidth=2.2, fontcolor="#D64545"];
    U -> R [label="  influences", color="#D64545", penwidth=2.2, fontcolor="#D64545"];
    A -> R [label="  causal effect", color="#2E7D32", penwidth=2.0, fontcolor="#2E7D32"];

    { rank=same; A; R; }
}
```
::::
:::

- **Complication in Sequential Settings**: Confounding compounds across time
  steps, distorting credit assignment over an entire trajectory
  - Confounding affects choice of $a_t$, which influences $s_{t+1}$, which
    influences future rewards $r_{t+k}$
  - Errors propagate through the trajectory

- **Common Source**: Logged or human-generated data
  - Original policy depended on unobserved factors
  - E.g., human driver brakes based on fatigue the RL agent can't sense

- **Risk**: Agent learns a policy that performs _worse than random_ once deployed

* Structural Causal Models for MDPs

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- **Definition**: A structural causal model (SCM) augments the MDP with explicit
  causal mechanisms
  - Equations describing how each variable is generated from its causes
  - Makes confounders, mediators, and colliders explicit
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

// TODO(ai_gp): Change colors so that different variables (noise, S_..., a_t,
// R_t) have the same color
```graphviz
digraph SCM {
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.8;
    ranksep=0.8;
    bgcolor="white";
    compound=true;

    graph [
        fontname="Helvetica",
        pad="0.25"
    ];

    node [
        shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=12,
        penwidth=1.4,
        margin="0.18,0.12"
    ];

    edge [
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=11,
        color="#4A4A4A",
        penwidth=1.4,
        arrowsize=0.8
    ];

    Us [label="U_s\n(State Noise)", fillcolor="#E8D9F7", color="#9B7DB1"];
    Ur [label="U_r\n(Reward Noise)", fillcolor="#E8D9F7", color="#9B7DB1"];
    St [label="S_t\n(State)", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C"];
    At [label="a_t\n(Action)", fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B"];
    St1 [label="S_{t+1}", fillcolor="#A0D6D1", color="#00897B"];
    Rt [label="R_t", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#1565C0"];

    Us -> St1 [penwidth=1.6];
    St -> St1 [penwidth=1.6];
    At -> St1 [penwidth=1.6];
    Ur -> Rt [penwidth=1.6];
    St -> Rt [penwidth=1.6];
    At -> Rt [penwidth=1.6];
    St1 -> Rt [penwidth=1.6];

    { rank=same; St; At; }
    { rank=same; St1; Rt; }
}
```
::::
:::

- **Formally**: An SCM specifies
// TODO(ai_gp): Use aligned with curly
  $$S_{t+1} := f_s(A_t, S_t, U_s)$$
  $$R_{t+1} := f_r(S_{t+1}, A_t, S_t, U_r)$$
  where $U_s, U_r$ are exogenous noise (unobserved causes)

- **Advantage over Standard MDPs**
  - Standard: $\Pr(s'|a,s)$ hides causal structure
  - SCM: explicit equations reveal _why_ $s'$ follows from $a$ and $s$

- **Enables Intervention**: The do-operator allows prediction under intervention
  $$\EE[R | do(a), s] = \EE[f_r(f_s(a, s, U_s), a, s, U_r)]$$
  instead of just conditioning $\EE[R | a, s]$ which is observational

* Counterfactual Reasoning and Credit Assignment

- **Intuition**: _"What would have happened had a different action been taken in
  this exact instance?"_, not just on average across similar states

- **Formalization**: Individual treatment effect (ITE)
  $$\tau_i(a_t) = R(a_t) - R(a'_t) \quad \text{for the same trajectory instance } i$$
  - Asks: _"on this exact trajectory, how much did action $a_t$ vs. $a'_t$ affect
    the final reward?"_
  - Notation: $R(a_t) | \text{do}(a_t=a)$ vs. $R(a'_t) | \text{do}(a_t=a')$

- **Benefits**
  - Enables learning from a single trajectory via unrealized alternatives
  - Avoids requiring repeated environment interaction
  - Improves credit assignment by isolating which action _actually caused_ a
    later reward vs merely _preceded it_

- **Key Idea**: Counterfactuals use the SCM to answer _"what if?"_ questions
  - E.g., _"If the robot had turned left at step $t$, would it have reached the
    goal?"_
  - Requires knowing causal mechanisms, not just observational statistics

* Deconfounding Offline / Off-Policy Data

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}

- **Motivation**: Offline RL trains on logged data from a different (often
  unknown) policy, exactly the setting where confounding is most likely!
  - Logging policy $\pi_{\text{log}}$ depends on unobserved factors
  - New policy $\pi_{\text{new}}$ may exploit spurious correlations and overfit
    to logging policy quirks rather than learning true environment dynamics

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}

```graphviz
digraph OfflineRL {
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.8;
    ranksep=0.8;
    bgcolor="white";
    compound=true;

    graph [
        fontname="Helvetica",
        pad="0.25"
    ];

    node [
        shape=box,
        style="rounded,filled",
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=12,
        penwidth=1.4,
        margin="0.18,0.12"
    ];

    edge [
        fontname="Helvetica",
        fontsize=11,
        penwidth=1.4,
        arrowsize=0.8
    ];

    U [label="Confounder U\n(logging policy depends on it)", fillcolor="#E8D9F7", color="#9B7DB1"];
    S [label="State s", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C"];
    A [label="Action a\n(log policy chose)", fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B"];
    R [label="Reward R", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", color="#558B2F"];

    U -> A [color="#D64545", penwidth=2.2, fontcolor="#D64545"];
    U -> R [color="#D64545", penwidth=2.2, fontcolor="#D64545"];
    S -> A [color="#4A4A4A"];
    A -> R [color="#4A4A4A"];
    S -> R [color="#4A4A4A"];

    { rank=same; S; A; }
}
```

// TODO(ai_gp): Mark red paths as "confounding backdoors"

::::
:::

- **Formalization**: Separate exploitable correlation from artifact
  - Data contains transitions $(s, a, r, s')$ generated by $\pi_{\text{log}}$
  - Need to estimate $\EE[R | do(a), s]$ from observational data
  - Confounding introduces bias:
    $\Pr_{\pi_{\text{log}}}(a | s) \neq \Pr_{\pi_{\text{new}}}(a | s)$

- **Solution**: Causal deconfounding methods use backdoor adjustment
  $$
  \EE[R | do(a), s] = \sum_u \Pr(u | s) \EE[R | a, s, u]
  $$
  - Condition on confounder $u$ to isolate causal effect


* Causal Generalization Across Environments

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}

- **Question**: Will a policy learned under environment $\mathcal{E}_1$ hold under
  environment $\mathcal{E}_2$?

- **Correlational Approach**: Surface similarity suggests transfer
  - Often fails: features that predict well in $\mathcal{E}_1$ may not in
    $\mathcal{E}_2$

// TODO(gp_ai) Add standard colors and make it look nicer
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph Transfer {
    rankdir=TB;
    splines=spline;
